Below is the step‐by‐step reasoning that led to the goal model and task decomposition.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Identify the mission’s main phases and requirements

• The mission requires a robot (or multiple robots) to “collect” resources from storage and “deliver” them to a requesting agent at a specified location.  
• In the collection phase the robot must (a) navigate to one or more storage locations (with the order defined by waiting time and path), (b) send a precise message to request the resources, and (c) wait for retrieval.  
• In the delivery phase the robot must (a) navigate to the delivery destination and (b) deliver the resource(s).  
• Both phases include battery‐management rules:
 – In the collection phase, if battery drops below 10% the robot must return to a recharging station and reassign the mission.
 – In the delivery phase, if battery falls below 30% the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining task; if that fails, an alert is triggered.
• Finally, if multiple resources (from different storages) are required, then the mission can be executed in parallel by multiple robots. This “multi‐robot coordination” is an alternative planning branch.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Decide on a tree structure

We propose a root goal (G1) for overall mission execution. Under G1 we define two main sequential phases:

 • G2: Collection Phase  
  – This phase further decomposes into (i) navigation to storage (G2.1), (ii) sending the request (G2.2), and (iii) battery management (G2.3).  
 • G3: Delivery Phase  
  – This phase decomposes into (i) navigation to the delivery destination (G3.1), (ii) resource delivery (G3.2), and (iii) battery management (G3.3).

Because the mission may require different robot assignments when multiple resources are involved, we add a separate goal (G4) for multi‐resource coordination. Under G4 we allow two alternatives:
 – G4.1: Single Robot Assignment (for a single resource)  
 – G4.2: Parallel Robot Assignment (for multiple resources)

For each goal that involves a conditional “if‐battery‐low” case we use a fallback notation. For example, in G2.3 the normal operation (G2.3.1) is the default, but if battery is low then G2.3.2 is executed. In the delivery phase (G3.3) we nest a further fallback if the checkpoint return fails.

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Assign goal types, runtime annotations, and logical relations

• The overall goal G1 is an Achieve goal (its target condition is “all required resources delivered”) and is executed sequentially (operator “;”) because collection must precede delivery.
• G2 and G3 are Perform goals (they specify a series of activities).
• The battery management goals (G2.3 and G3.3) use the FALLBACK operator because if the battery is sufficient the normal branch is executed; otherwise an alternative (or nested alternative) is taken.
• The multi‐resource coordination goal G4 is a Perform goal whose children represent an OR decomposition (the mission may be performed by a single robot or by parallel robots). For the runtime annotation we choose “#” (parallel execution) to indicate that the alternatives are available concurrently. (Note: In our notation the OR relation is indicated in the “Relation” column, while the runtime annotation uses the available symbols; here “#” is used to denote that alternatives can be executed in parallel if selected.)

──────────────────────────────
Step 4. Define the tasks

We define concrete tasks for the atomic actions:
 – AT1: Navigate to Storage (executed by a single robot at the storage facility).  
 – AT2: Request Resource (executed at the storage facility).  
 – AT3: Navigate to Delivery Location (executed by a single robot at the delivery destination).  
 – AT4: Deliver Resource (executed at the delivery destination).  

Each task is labeled with its location and the number of robots (in these cases “1” because each action is performed by the assigned robot).

